---
<BaseLayout
  extraJsonLd={[techArticleJsonLd]}
  title="Mud Pit / Solids Control System: Shakers, Desanders"
  description="How a rig's solids control system works: shale shakers, desanders and desilters, degassers, and why drilling fluid has to be processed before it can be reused."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Equipment', href: '/equipment' }, { label: 'Mud Pit System', href: '/equipment/mud-pit-system' }]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/equipment/mud-pit-system"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/equipment/mud-pit-system" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Equipment — Core Systems</div>
    <h1>Mud Pit / Solids <span>Control System</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">Shale shakers, desanders, desilters, and degassers that clean drilled solids and gas out of returned drilling fluid before it goes back down the hole.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="what-it-is">What This System Does</h2>
        <p>Drilling fluid returning from the <a href="/equipment/fluid-circulation-system">circulation system</a> is loaded with everything it just carried out of the hole — cuttings ranging from coarse chunks down to fine colloidal particles, and sometimes entrained gas. Left in the fluid, that loading increases viscosity and density in ways that are hard to control, accelerates wear on the mud pumps, and steadily reduces how well the fluid can carry new cuttings on its next trip down and back. The solids control system exists to pull that material back out so the same fluid can be reused rather than replaced.</p>
        <p>The processed fluid lands back in horizontal cylindrical mud tanks, and reading pit volume off a sight glass or strapping chart only works if you can actually convert a fill height to a volume for that specific tank — see the <a href="/calculators/horizontal-tank-capacity">horizontal tank capacity calculator</a> for that lookup.</p>

        <KeyFacts
          lang="en"
          rows={[["Processing order", "Shale shakers → degasser → desander → desilter → centrifuge (optional). The degasser commonly sits just downstream of the shakers so gas-cut mud never reaches the centrifugal pumps feeding the cones."], ["Desander cones", "Typically 6\" to 12\" diameter; removes sand-sized solids, roughly 40 microns and larger."], ["Desilter cones", "Typically 2\" to 5\" diameter (4\" is common); removes silt-sized solids, roughly 15 to 40 microns."], ["Centrifuge", "Removes the finest solids, down to roughly 2 to 5 microns, or recovers barite and base fluid on weighted and oil-based systems."], ["Shaker screens", "Labelled by API number under API RP 13C (for example API 140) rather than the old “mesh” count."], ["Pits on the system", "Active (suction) pits, reserve pits, slug pit and trip tank, with pit volume totalizer (PVT) sensors for kick detection."]]}
          standards={["api13c"]}
        />

        <h2 id="shakers">Shale Shakers</h2>
        <p>Shale shakers are the first stage in the sequence and handle the bulk of the volume: vibrating screens that returns flow across, catching the coarsest cuttings while letting fluid and finer particles pass through into the pits below. Screen mesh size is a real, adjustable trade-off — finer screens catch more solids but process fluid more slowly and wear out faster; coarser screens move more volume but let more fine solids through to the next stage.</p>

        <h2 id="hydrocyclones">Desanders and Desilters</h2>
        <p>What passes through the shaker screens moves on to <strong>desanders</strong>, then <strong>desilters</strong> — both are <strong>hydrocyclones</strong>, cone-shaped vessels with no internal moving parts at all. Fluid enters tangentially near the top and is forced into a tight spiral; centrifugal force throws denser solid particles outward to the cone wall, where they spiral down and discharge out the bottom (the underflow), while cleaned fluid exits up through the center (the overflow). Desanders and desilters are the same basic device at different scale — smaller-diameter cones spin the fluid faster and separate progressively finer particles, which is the only real difference between the two.</p>

        <h2 id="degassers">Degassers</h2>
        <p>Gas entrained in returning mud — whether from a real kick or just gas liberated from cuttings — makes the fluid lighter and less effective at both hole cleaning and holding back formation pressure, on top of the general well control concerns covered on the <a href="/drilling/well-control">well control page</a>. A degasser removes that entrained gas from the fluid at whatever point in the processing sequence suits the specific fluid system, restoring the mud closer to its real, intended density before it's recirculated.</p>

        <h2 id="centrifuges">Centrifuges — the Optional Extra Stage</h2>
        <p>A centrifuge is a higher-speed, mechanically-driven separator for the finest solids remaining after the hydrocyclone stages — finer than desanders and desilters alone can efficiently pull out. It's not a universal requirement on every well; it shows up most often on jobs running more expensive fluid systems, particularly oil-based mud, where recovering and reusing the base fluid rather than discarding it along with the fine solids has a real cost justification behind the extra processing step.</p>

        <h2 id="portable-tanks">Portable Tanks Aren't Part of This System</h2>
        <p>The pits and processing equipment above are rectangular, built into the mud system itself. A location's portable fuel, water, or chemical tanks are a different, simpler class of vessel entirely — usually a horizontal cylinder on a skid, with none of the shaker/hydrocyclone processing this page covers. See the <a href="/calculators/horizontal-tank-capacity">horizontal tank capacity calculator</a> for turning a dipstick reading on one of those into an actual volume.</p>

      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="toc">
